1. Introduction to Handling Missing Data
The Problem: Most machine learning algorithms (like those in scikit-learn) are not capable of handling missing data during training
.
Responsibility: It is the data scientist's responsibility to remove or fill missing values before feeding data into a model
.
General Strategies:
Removal: Deleting the rows or columns containing missing values
.
Imputation: Filling in the missing values
.
Univariate Imputation: Focusing on one column at a time (e.g., using mean, median, mode, or random values)
.
Multivariate Imputation: Using other available features to predict missing values (e.g., KNN Imputer or Iterative Imputer/MICE)
.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
2. Complete Case Analysis (CCA)
CCA, also known as "listwise deletion," is the process of discarding entire observations (rows) where any of the variables (columns) are missing
.
Key Assumptions
MCAR (Missing Completely At Random): The primary assumption for CCA is that data is missing completely at random
.
Distribution Preservation: If data is MCAR, removing rows should not change the statistical distribution of the remaining data compared to the original dataset
.
When to Use CCA
When you are confident the data is missing randomly
.
The 5% Rule: Generally, CCA is preferred only when the missing data in a column is less than 5% of the total observations
.
Note: If a column has an extremely high percentage of missing data (e.g., 95%), you should consider removing the entire column rather than individual rows
.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
3. Advantages and Disadvantages
Advantages
Disadvantages
Very easy to implement (e.g., using dropna() in pandas)
.
Can lead to a massive loss of data if many rows have at least one missing value
.
No complex data manipulation required
.
May discard informative observations if data is not missing at random
.
Preserves the distribution of variables (if MCAR)
.
Production Risk: The model never learns how to handle missing data. If a missing value appears during real-world inference (production), the model won't know how to treat it
.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
4. Practical Implementation & Validation
When performing CCA, you must verify that the data distribution has not been significantly altered.
For Numerical Data:
Plot Histograms or Probability Density Functions (PDF) for the variable before and after applying CCA
.
If the distributions overlap almost perfectly, the CCA is valid
.
For Categorical Data:
Calculate the ratio/proportion of each category within the column before and after CCA
.
The relative frequencies (e.g., the percentage of "Graduates" vs. "Masters") should remain stable
.

